var e={codeDescription:`JavaScript function body: read input, await operations, return JSON-serializable data. For browser workflows choose domain client (not server/both). context.browser provides await openTab({url, active:false, keepOpen:false}), openView({route, active:true, keepOpen:true}), getTab(tabId), navigate(tabId,url), waitForSelector(tabId,selector,{timeoutMs:10000}), extract(tabId,{selector:'body',format:'text'|'html'|'links',maxChars:50000,timeoutMs:10000}), click(tabId,selector), fill(tabId,selector,value), closeTab(tabId). openTab/openView return {id,url,title}; extract returns {url,title,content}. Use tab.id, not tab.tabId. Only tabs opened by this execution are accessible. HTTP(S) pages only; Chrome site permissions and protected-page restrictions apply. No arbitrary script evaluation or raw chrome APIs. Click/fill require risk write. Temporary tabs auto-close on success/error; keepOpen true preserves tabs for the user. Extension views cannot be scraped or clicked. Calls should be sequential; selector waits are capped at 30 seconds, sessions at 10 minutes, 10 tabs maximum. OpenView route is a registered static extension route, e.g. /scripts, /suiteql, /custom-tools, /dashboards. Browser-only tools may use modules: []. Example: const tab = await context.browser.openTab({url:input.url}); await context.browser.waitForSelector(tab.id, '.results'); return await context.browser.extract(tab.id,{selector:'.results',format:'text'}); Provide concrete testInput, test in client domain after create/update, report results, and leave activation to the user.`};export{e as t};